    "transcript": [
      {
        "speaker": "S1",
        "text": "Good morning, everyone, and welcome to Harlow Castle. My name is Paul and I'll be your guide for the next ninety minutes. Before we begin, a few practical points. The tour includes the main hall, the towers and the gardens. Please stay with the group, and don't touch the old furniture. Photography is allowed everywhere except in the chapel, because the flash can damage the paintings."
      },
      {
        "speaker": "S1",
        "text": "The castle was built in 1290 by Sir William Harlow, who was a knight in the army of King Edward the First. At first it was a simple wooden building, but in the fourteenth century the family rebuilt it in stone. You can still see the original wall in the east corner of the courtyard. The castle was never attacked successfully, although a large army tried in 1461. The attackers waited for six weeks, but they went home when the winter arrived."
      },
      {
        "speaker": "S1",
        "text": "In the main hall, which we'll visit first, you'll see the long table where the family ate. It could seat forty people. The big fireplace is the original one, but the carpet is only about a hundred years old. Next, we'll climb the north tower. There are one hundred and twelve steps, so if anyone has problems with stairs, there's a lift in the south tower instead. From the top, on a clear day, you can see the sea, which is about ten miles away."
      },
      {
        "speaker": "S1",
        "text": "After the towers, we'll go to the gardens. They were designed in the eighteenth century and they're famous for the roses. They're at their best in June, so you're lucky today. We'll finish at the café at about twelve thirty. The cakes are made in the castle kitchen and I can really recommend the lemon cake. If you want to buy souvenirs, the gift shop closes at five o'clock. Now, shall we begin?"
      }
    ]
  },
